def exist(board, word):
    rows, cols = len(board), len(board[0])

    def dfs(r, c, i):
        if i == len(word):
            return True
        if not (0 <= r < rows and 0 <= c < cols) or board[r][c] != word[i]:
            return False
        temp, board[r][c] = board[r][c], '#'    # mark visited in place
        found = (dfs(r+1, c, i+1) or dfs(r-1, c, i+1) or
                 dfs(r, c+1, i+1) or dfs(r, c-1, i+1))
        board[r][c] = temp                        # restore before returning
        return found

    return any(dfs(r, c, 0) for r in range(rows) for c in range(cols))
```

Overwriting `board[r][c]` with a sentinel character that can never match a real letter is the "choose" step; restoring it with `temp` right before returning — whether the recursive search succeeded or not — is "unexplore." Skipping the restore corrupts the board for every sibling call that hasn't run yet.
